findings.body:
`listFeaturesForUser` ends with `.filter((state) => !state.globalEnabled)`. This inverts the intended filter. The docstring says it returns features that are "globally enabled", and the previous code used `state.globalEnabled`.

With this change, the Features page at `/settings/my-account/features` lists only features that are globally switched off, which users cannot act on. Every globally enabled opt-in feature disappears from the page.

::diff{file="packages/features/feature-opt-in/services/FeatureOptInService.ts" lines="191-208"}

::suggestion{file="packages/features/feature-opt-in/services/FeatureOptInService.ts" lines="207-207"}
return featureIds.map((featureId) => resolvedStates[featureId]).filter((state) => state.globalEnabled);

Also add a unit test that covers this method. There is currently none, which is why this went unnoticed.

`setUserFeatureState` now checks `if (state !== "inherit")`, which is inverted. The branch that passes no `assignedBy` runs for `enabled`/`disabled`, and the branch that passes `assignedBy: "user-<id>"` runs for `inherit`.

The `FeaturesRepository.setUserFeatureState` enabled path reads `input.assignedBy` for the upsert. Here that value is `undefined`, so the write either fails or stores a null `assignedBy`. The inherit path passes an `assignedBy` the repository ignores. The existing team variant (`setTeamFeatureState`) uses `state === "inherit"` correctly, so this is a regression on the user side only.

::diff{file="packages/features/feature-opt-in/services/FeatureOptInService.ts" lines="250-275"}

::suggestion{file="packages/features/feature-opt-in/services/FeatureOptInService.ts" lines="264-274"}
