`listFeaturesForUser` has no unit test in this PR, even though it gained a scope filter and a changed final predicate. The inverted `globalEnabled` filter from the other finding would have been caught by a single test that checks an enabled user-scoped feature is returned and an org-only feature is excluded.

The PR's own test plan says to verify that `/settings/my-account/features` shows user-scoped features only. Nothing automated backs that up.

::diff{file="packages/features/feature-opt-in/services/FeatureOptInService.ts" lines="191-208"}

The integration test replaces `isFeatureAllowedForScope` with `() => true` for the whole file. Its comment says scope validation is covered by unit tests, but the integration suite is also the only place that runs `setUserFeatureState` / `setTeamFeatureState` against a real database. Nothing there checks that a scope-restricted feature is rejected.

The mock also works against the production behavior: a user-scoped feature set through the database path would never hit the real check. Prefer configuring the feature with the right `scope` in a test fixture, or use a real `OPT_IN_FEATURES` entry, over stubbing the guard.

::diff{file="packages/features/feature-opt-in/services/FeatureOptInService.integration-test.ts" lines="11-19"}

`listFeaturesForTeam` and `setTeamFeatureState` both default `scope` to `"team"`. The router passes it explicitly today. But any future caller that forgets `scope` on the org path (for example, a new org-level endpoint) will silently validate and filter against team scope, not fail.

This is also one of the human-review checklist items. A required parameter on the service would make the choice explicit, and the default could live in the router.

